Hi @RLWA32,

Thanks for asking. In my same-process tests, I was able to reproduce the write results you described for all three VirtualAlloc protection settings.

I allocated three regions using VirtualAlloc, initially with PAGE_READWRITE, and then changed two of them to PAGE_READONLY and PAGE_EXECUTE_READ using VirtualProtect.

Here are the results:

```text
Handle: PROCESS_VM_WRITE only
PAGE_READWRITE                 ok=0  written=0  error=5  value=0x11
PAGE_READONLY                  ok=0  written=0  error=5  value=0x22
PAGE_EXECUTE_READ              ok=0  written=0  error=5  value=0x33

Handle: PROCESS_VM_WRITE | PROCESS_VM_OPERATION
PAGE_READWRITE                 ok=1  written=1  error=0  value=0xA1
PAGE_READONLY                  ok=0  written=0  error=998  value=0x22
PAGE_EXECUTE_READ              ok=1  written=1  error=0  value=0xC1
```

With PROCESS_VM_WRITE only, all three writes failed with ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED. When PROCESS_VM_OPERATION was also included, writes to PAGE_READWRITE and PAGE_EXECUTE_READ succeeded, while the write to PAGE_READONLY failed with ERROR_NOACCESS.

My original cross-process reproduction was limited to a PAGE_READWRITE region allocated with VirtualAlloc, where WriteProcessMemory succeeded without PROCESS_VM_OPERATION. I haven't yet tested memory allocated using HeapAlloc, GlobalAlloc, or LocalAlloc, or variables in their own segments. I also haven't repeated all three protection tests across separate processes. These tests cover WriteProcessMemory only.
